contentText:
In April, Hongjoong returned to Coachella with nothing to do.

Two years earlier, he had been on the Indio Valley stage when ATEEZ became the first K-pop boy band to perform at the festival. This time he was a guest, watching other people work. &#8220;That day I got so many inspirations,&#8221; he says of a trip where he linked up with Teddy Swims , PinkPantheress , Central Cee , Swae Lee and Daesung of BIGBANG . &#8220;I really love to experience everywhere.&#8221; He came back to Los Angeles, booked a session and crafted an atmospheric pop song that he specifies is &#8220;not just chill&#8221; but about a feeling he had been chasing his whole life.

That song arrives Friday (Sept. 18) as &#8220;ENOuGH,&#8221; a collaboration with Bazzi , that is unhurried, openly emotional and, perhaps most notably, sounds nothing like the group he leads in the midst of their biggest season yet.

On June 26, ATEEZ unleashed GOLDEN HOUR : Part.5 and made a headlining debut at London&#8217;s BST Hyde Park in the U.K. just two days later. The EP gave the group its third No. 1 on the Billboard 200 , opening with a career-high 228,000 equivalent album units. At home in Korea, Part.5 &#8216;s lead single &#8220;BAD&#8221; climbed the local charts thanks in part to a viral dance, helping ATEEZ secure their first entry on the Billboard Global 200 . Next month, a full day dedicated to ATEEZ and their fans at the inaugural 82WORLD: LA festival is already sold out.

But none of that superstardom is visible when Hongjoong joins an afternoon video call in August from a sun-filled Los Angeles hotel room. He&#8217;s dressed down in a plain white V-neck T-shirt with the collar cut off, a few silver rings stacked on different fingers, and a single fingernail painted black — a signature he rarely goes without to raise awareness for the Polished Man campaign, which aims to end violence against children. He looks less like the leader of one of K-pop&#8217;s hardest-working groups than someone enjoying a rare stretch of good weather with nowhere urgent to be. &#8220;We are so busy these days,&#8221; he says, &#8220;but finally I get to chill a little here.&#8221;

So, when the trending group of the moment&#8217;s leader turns up with a subtle song of his own, it&#8217;s important to note that it&#8217;s not a full-fledged solo departure. &#8220;If I give you a new solo debut, maybe I (will have) prepared more than one year,&#8221; he says. &#8220;But for this one, I made this song in April in L.A.&#8221; A proper solo debut would require far more than the few months it took to deliver &#8220;ENOuGH&#8221; — a song made on a break, at a moment when, for the first time in a long time, he wasn&#8217;t sure what he was making.

&#8220;I just tried to make some chill song and also not just chill,&#8221; he says of those April studio days. &#8220;Before starting the music in L.A. in the session, before this song [became] &#8216;ENOuGH,&#8217; I just focused on making good sounds because I think I need that in here. But for this song, I had a very specific message I wanted to write, and we talked about that before starting the session&#8230;I always walked with ATEEZ, and I’m really happy with that. At the same time, I&#8217;m 27 now, and I did eight years with ATEEZ. I made so many songs for ATEEZ and myself too, and now I think I have to get more experience because doing that will reflect to ATEEZ again and myself. So, that&#8217;s why I started this project and that&#8217;s why I think this song is very much &#8216;enough&#8217; to start a collaboration project.&#8221;

Kim Hongjoong has spent almost a decade as ATEEZ&#8217;s leader, as well as a consistent songwriter and in-house producer for the act with nearly 200 songs officially registered under his name. In May, he made his debut as music director on the indie film Hallucination , which screened at the Jeonju International Film Festival; weeks later, he appeared on the K-POPS! soundtrack for a track alongside Anderson .Paak and Jay Park; and next month, he&#8217;ll make his solo festival debut via his first-ever live DJ set under the moniker NO1 at EDC Korea.

Still, by Hongjoong&#8217;s account, the year started confusingly.

&#8220;Last year, I really thought I knew myself very well because I know what I can do and where my good points and bad points are,&#8221; he shares. &#8220;But at the start of this year, I felt I was still learning about myself because if I make songs, sometimes I use high notes and sometimes I use low notes, but I thought that my high note is good because my vocal tone is very high and I think I&#8217;ll just use that when I make songs. But at the start of this year, when I listened to the songs that I made, I thought everything was not good. And also it makes me not &#8216;enough&#8217; because — and I don&#8217;t know why — artists always change their flavor and music types.&#8221;

That reckoning and realization sent him looking for the gap between what he&#8217;s become good at as a musician and what made him want to be one in the first place.

&#8220;I thought I have to learn myself more with ATEEZ and also without ATEEZ too because I want to know about me, what I really like, and what I really don&#8217;t like,&#8221; he continues. &#8220;That&#8217;s why this music style is quite different than what I made before and ATEEZ&#8217;s style. I really love ATEEZ&#8217;s style, and I know I&#8217;m really good at doing a charismatic rap, but at the same time, the songs that I listened to when I was young were almost all emotional songs. So, that&#8217;s why I started this song with the kind of emotional chords, but also the very unique and comfortable sound.&#8221;

He&#8217;s not wrong about the charisma (listen to him declare himself the captain on &#8220;Say My Name,&#8221; the fire he spits on &#8220;This World&#8221; or last year&#8217;s solo cut &#8220;NO1&#8221;), but he&#8217;s also careful not to plant his personal flag in any one genre.

&#8220;In the near future, [my style] can change, so I want to try to collab with more artists,&#8221; he says. &#8220;Or if I release more songs, I think many things within a year will really change because I&#8217;m still learning myself. If I get my own style or learn my own style, I think there will be someday I can talk to you to say, &#8216;This is my style.&#8217; I&#8217;m not sure this is my real style, but these days, at least in April, I felt this is one of my styles.&#8221;

Hongjoong

Tyler Shields

Hongjoong&#8217;s opportunity to go find out came from something he hadn&#8217;t experienced in years: time off.

Late last year, he and his ATEEZ members sat down with management company, KQ Entertainment, and discussed setting two vacation periods in 2026. &#8220;We didn&#8217;t have any vacation before this year,&#8221; he shares. &#8220;When we confirmed to go on vacation, I immediately thought about how I could use that vacation for me and how I could use the vacation for loving myself and expressing myself at the same time.&#8221;

All eight ATEEZ members re-signed with KQ for another seven years in July 2025, with the second chapter leaving more space for individuality: Mingi released a six-track solo EP in May, Yunho has moved more into acting and others are taking on their own individual work, which Hongjoong frames as crucial maintenance for the group. &#8220;We put ATEEZ first,&#8221; he says. &#8220;But at the same time, everyone wants to plan their own things too. I think that&#8217;s very important to make ATEEZ last longer because everyone needs to express themselves and that makes synergy.&#8221;

